Now main.js. Handle init, loading, start, resume, error overlay, touch detection.

Also need to hide loading after Game constructed. Since constructor builds world synchronously, DOM won't repaint before; use requestAnimationFrame after showing loading. main.js:
```
import { Game } from './game.js';
const canvas = document.getElementById('game');
const loading = ...
let game;
function boot() {
  try {
    game = new Game(canvas);
    window.game = game;
    loading.classList.add('hidden');
    startBtn.disabled=false;
  } catch(e) { show error; }
}
requestAnimationFrame(()=>setTimeout(boot,50));
```
Actually top-level module executes after DOM; constructor ~1s. Loading text initially visible. Use double rAF.

start button:
```
startBtn.addEventListener('click', ()=> { if(!game) return; game.start(); document.body.classList.add('playing'); });
```
game.start hides menu.

resume button: canvas.requestPointerLock.

Also if pointer lock fails (headless), start still works; paused may be set true by pointerlockchange? If request fails, no change event; paused remains false. Mouse look won't work without lock; for screenshots fine. For real users okay.

Handle WebGL context failure: display message.

Add keyboard Enter to start.

Add orientation hint for mobile? optional.

Let's write.

