content.text:
Now test again. Also note `stats.playerKills` now only from player, but `e._killedByPlayer` remains true if player hits but doesn't kill; then AI kills later -> feed says player killed. Minor. We can clear flag on death? It's okay; if player damaged them, credit is plausible. But if player hit a soldier once and AI kills, feed says "ВЫ". Not terrible.

Now think about AI plane deaths: both bot planes die in ~2 min. Maybe okay. But a user might want to see planes in the air; if they both crash quickly, sky is empty. Could improve AI plane flight safety and reduce crashes. Why do they die? Possibly they crash into terrain because AI dives at ground targets. Let's analyze. After takeoff, AI picks target (enemy plane or ground). For ground target, it flies toward it at low altitude and may hit ground. `alt < 22` sets pitch 0.9; should climb. But when targeting ground, local.y is negative, `p.controls.pitch = clamp(local.y*2.2, -0.85,0.9)` at alt>22 dives; at alt<22 climbs. Should avoid ground. Yet maybe due to bank angle, plane rolls and loses altitude. Or `heightAt` at plane position and `altitude` can be small near hills. Both planes may collide with each other or be shot down by infantry/tank MGs. Stats enemy=5, allied=25; plane kills likely. Fine.

Maybe add respawn of planes? Currently destroyed planes stay dead forever. The game would lose air action after ~2 min. User asked aviation; better to respawn bot planes after some delay. Could implement plane respawn: in updateRespawns or ai plane timer, if a bot plane dead after 45s, reset it at base. But resetting complex: need restore group, alive, health, modules, position, pilot, spawn a pilot soldier. Could add method `Plane.reset()` and AI logic. This is substantial but doable.

Alternatively, don't destroy bot planes easily: increase health or reduce crashes. But eventually they die. The game session may be short. Could leave.

Similarly tanks: they survive. Good.

Maybe add plane respawn for continuous gameplay:
- In AIController.update, check for dead planes with `destroyedAt` > 60 and team; reset them at spawn points and spawn a bot pilot soldier.
- Need `Plane.reset(x,z,yaw,team)`:
  - set alive=true, crashed=false, crashing=false, health=180, modules all 100, pilot=null, pilotSoldier=null, bailed=false, onGround=true, throttle=0, vel=0, yaw, quat, pos y=heightAt+0.06, group visible, topWing visible, rotation reset, group position/quat; ammo=400.
  - Existing bot pilot soldier is dead; spawn new Soldier at nearby with aiRole pilot boardTarget plane.
- Could do in game updateRespawns? Let's implement in AIController:
```
this.planeRespawnT = 0;
...
for (const p of game.planes) {
 if (!p.alive && p.destroyedAt && game.time - p.destroyedAt > 55) this.respawnPlane(p);
}
```
But `game.time` doesn't advance in manual simulation because we don't increment; in real game it does. Better use a timer `p.deadTimer` incremented in update. Add in Plane.update dead branch `this.deadTimer += dt`. Then AI if deadTimer>55 reset.
- `respawnPlane(p)` determine spawn based on team: British playerPlane at -368,-103? If it's the player's plane and destroyed, resetting it lets player use again. Bot plane at -400,-110. German at 352,118. Use WORLD? ai imports WORLD. We have positions.
- Spawn a pilot soldier for bot planes only (not player plane). For player plane, no pilot; player can enter. For enemy plane, spawn German pilot and set boardTarget.
- If p === game.playerPlane, reset without pilot. If p === game.botPlane, spawn British pilot. If p === game.enemyPlane, spawn German.
- Need to clear old pilot soldier? It's already dead.
- `Plane.reset` should also remove existing group? We reuse group. Ensure `group.visible = true`; topWing visible.
- The plane group may have crashed rotation z=1.2; reset group.quaternion.

This would keep air combat alive. Nice.

Also tank respawn? Tanks likely survive; if destroyed, no respawn. Could add later.

Let's implement Plane.reset and AI respawn. But careful: `game.playerPlane` may be dead while player is not in it; resetting it is good. `destroyedAt` uses game.time, which in real loop advances. Add `deadTimer` for robustness.

In Plane constructor: `this.deadTimer = 0`.
